  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Delhi protests: SC says no illegal detention, orders Metro, internet back",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/delhi-protests-sc-says-no-illegal-detention-orders-metro-internet-back-126101100233_1.html",
      "description": "The Supreme Court on Sunday directed that people gathering peacefully should not be illegally detained, as it issued a notice to the Centre on a plea seeking contempt proceedings for alleged violation of the court's direction to ensure that there is no \"complete prohibition\" on metro and train services in Delhi due to the CJP protest.\nIn a special sitting, a bench of justices Joymalya Bagchi and V Mohana asked Solicitor General Tushar Mehta to ensure that Delhi Metro functions as per its normal schedule, and that there is no disruption to internet services, subject to law and order conditions.\n\"In view of the urgent applications alleging violation of our earlier order dt. October 9, 2026, and also alleging illegal detention of large number of individuals at Delhi on October 10, 2026 this court had assembled for an urgent hearing.\n\"Senior counsels submitted there was a flagrant violation of the directions -- metro railway was not functioning, New Delhi and Nizamuddin railway stations",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-11T07:31:36"
    },
    {
      "title": "SC to hear all lawyers in plea over Delhi Metro curbs during CJP protest",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/sc-to-hear-all-lawyers-in-plea-over-delhi-metro-curbs-during-cjp-protest-126101100188_1.html",
      "description": "In a special sitting, the Supreme Court on Sunday said it will grant hearing to all lawyers while dealing with a plea seeking contempt proceedings against authorities for alleged blatant violation of the court's direction to ensure that \"complete prohibition\" of metro and trains services should not be implemented in Delhi due to the CJP protest.\nA bench of justices Joymalya Bagchi and V Mohana said it will hear all parties and ensure no inconvenience is caused, saying the top court is always at the service of the nation.\n\"We know you all are aggrieved by what happened yesterday. We are not here to trivialise what happened,\" the bench said.\nThe top court is also hearing a habeas petition filed by senior advocate Menaka Guruswamy against the detention of political leaders.\nThe contempt plea, filed by advocate Shailendra Mani Tripathi, has arrayed Union Home Secretary Govind Mohan, Delhi Police Commissioner Anurag Kumar and Delhi Metro Rail Corporation chairperson D Thara as ...",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-11T06:36:37"
    }
  ]
